Subject: A save log: one stderr line per change to a file under UDATA

A save-point save writes several files under the save folder's UDATA; a process ending between them
leaves a set the game reports as damaged (research/qwen/notes/q22-saves.md). To measure that window in
any run or player's log, each close of a write-opened UDATA file, each delete and each shrink logs
"[FCI] save @<tick>: <path> ...", the path being the Xbox path (backslash separators) relative to the
save folder. A write-opened file closed with no writes, whose open changed nothing, leaves no line; if
the open created the file or truncated an existing one (the create disposition and whether the file
existed before the open tell), the close line says so: "created, ..." / "truncated, ...". Read-only
opens and other files leave no line. No title issues a class-10 rename (measured, Instincts): the
level-start save deletes the free slot and writes <level>.fcs fresh, and a re-save over an existing
.fcs rewrites it in place -- no rename, no overwrite of a live name; the rename case is kept only so a
title that does rename leaves a line. Runs of path separators the game doubles are collapsed, so a
delete reads 9FBDF33E7A0C\fcs0.fcs, not 9FBDF33E7A0C\\fcs0.fcs.

The filter recognises both spellings of the save partition, because a title may open the same files
either way and the log must not go quiet on half a save: the device path
\Device\Harddisk0\PartitionN\UDATA\... and the U: drive letter (U:\..., case-insensitive, an
object-manager \??\ prefix transparent). This is what the title's profile folder is opened through:
the save code opens 9FBDF33E7A0C\profile.sav and friends as u:\... (the slot path stored in
profile.sav itself is "u:\9FBDF33E7A0C\"), which the device-path-only filter used to miss.

The guest's kernel calls enter through kernel_bridge.c, so the hooks sit where the bridge takes the
host call: create in bridge_NtCreateFile/bridge_NtOpenFile via bridge_create_file_impl into
xbox_NtCreateFile, write in bridge_NtWriteFile into xbox_NtWriteFile, set-EOF/disposition/rename in
bridge_NtSetInformationFile into xbox_NtSetInformationFile, delete in bridge_NtDeleteFile into
xbox_NtDeleteFile, and close in bridge_NtClose itself -- the guest's NtClose closes the host handle
directly and never reaches xbox_NtClose, so savelog_close is called there (kernel.h declares it,
kernel_file.c defines it non-static). The bridge passes the same host handle savelog_open recorded:
bridge_create_file_impl stores it with bridge_write_handle, bridge_NtClose takes it back with
bridge_take_handle. The tracking table's lock is a static SRWLOCK (SRWLOCK_INIT), race-free from first
use on both backends, instead of a lazy InitializeCriticalSection that races when two threads make the
first file call.

